 the korean  bestsellers are also susceptible to a  lot of marketing for example right now  the ewg is a really big thing in korean  marketing and  as you hopefully know from watching my  channel  their stuff is just complete  pseudoscience it's okay to miss a day of  wearing sunscreen yes i completely agree  with this getting really anxious about  missing a single day of sunscreen is  usually not really worth it obviously  i'm not talking about like a 12-hour day  where you're running around on the beach  and getting massively sunburned that is  probably not a good idea and if you are  getting severely sunburned you are  increasing your risk of skin cancer but  if you're just having very low uv  exposure that day like maybe you're  going to the office or you had a short  walk in the winter then it's probably  fine you've probably skipped a lot of  days of sunscreen before you discovered  skin care one extra day of skipping it  is not going to set you all the way back  if you do find yourself skipping a lot  of days though then it might be good to  consider if you have a backup plan it  might be good to think about how you can  develop a habit of wearing sunscreen you  might want to keep some sun protective  clothing in your car or keep sunscreen  in your handbag just as much  misinformation comes from chemists as  well i think this comment is saying  compared to dermatologists and i would  disagree not because i think chemists  are more correct or right all the time  but just because there are a lot less  chemists saying things on large  platforms i have definitely seen some  chemists saying very incorrect stuff in  magazine articles and on social media i  actually read a bunch of sunscreen  magazine articles recently for an  instagram post and  it was pretty bad but for every chemist  i came across who was interviewed there  were like 25 dermatologists interviewed  so just based on the volume alone i  don't think it is possible for chemists  to be contributing as 